 First, we know that an obtuse triangle has one angle that is greater than 90 degrees. Since the problem states that one angle is 50% larger than 90 degrees, we can compute this angle as follows: \[
90 \text{ degrees} \times (1 + 0.50) = 90 \times 1.5 = 135 \text{ degrees}
\] The sum of the interior angles of any triangle is always 180 degrees. Thus, the sum of the remaining two angles in this obtuse triangle must be: \[
180 \text{ degrees} - 135 \text{ degrees} = 45 \text{ degrees}
\] Since the triangle is isosceles, it has two equal angles. Let's denote the measure of one of these angles as \( x \). Then, we can write the equation: \[
2x = 45 \text{ degrees}
\] Solving for \( x \), we get: \[
x = \frac{45}{2} = 22.5 \text{ degrees}
\] Therefore, the measure of one of the two smallest angles in the triangle is \boxed{22.5} degrees.

